  "response": "A substation transformer failure admits two broadly different origins: insulation that has deteriorated internally over years of service, and an external transient that stresses the winding in microseconds. Where the chemical evidence from the oil and the electrical record of the event appear to favour different answers, the investigation proceeds by establishing which channel of evidence is genuinely diagnostic and which is merely consistent with either origin.\n\nOil samples are drawn promptly after a failure to preserve the dissolved gas concentrations. Only once that sampling has been completed does dissolved gas analysis follow, which quantifies the gases dissolved in a transformer's insulating oil.\n\nAcetylene in transformer oil indicates high-energy arcing inside the tank. Acetylene is generated in large quantities by normal thermal aging of oil below 150 degrees Celsius, and this cannot be squared with the arcing signature, since a gas that marks a high-energy discharge cannot at the same time be an abundant product of ordinary low-temperature aging.\n\nIEC 60076-7 is the standard that governs the interpretation of dissolved gas analysis results. IEEE C57.104 provides guidance for interpreting gases generated in oil-immersed transformers, and because the interpretation of gas results cannot be governed by two different documents at once, the role assigned to IEC 60076-7 is ruled out. That guidance in IEEE C57.104 is precisely what leads investigators to compare archived pre-failure gas trends with post-failure gas levels, and such comparison separates gradual degradation from a sudden fault. Whereas that comparison reconstructs the chemistry over months of service, digital fault recorders capture time-stamped voltage and current waveforms during the event itself. SCADA sequence-of-event logs establish the timing of nearby circuit breaker operations relative to the failure, and a breaker operation logged at the very instant of failure is what shows the surge mechanism to have been in play. Surge arrester counters record the number of discharge operations at the transformer terminals, a tally brought about by switching surges impressing steep-fronted transient overvoltages on the line-end turns of the winding. Teardown inspection of the core and windings comes later in this sequence, after the waveforms of the event have been captured, and it locates the physical point of failure origin.\n\nThe determination therefore rests on convergence rather than on any single indicator. A defensible attribution of origin requires the chemical signature of the oil, the electrical record of the event, and the physical condition of the active part to describe one and the same failure mechanism, and any channel that stands alone against the others is held to require explanation before a cause is declared.",
